\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Remark}
\title{Global coefficients retain singular backgrounds}
\date{2026-11-17}

\p{The fiber trace of \ref{fdip-00WG} gives, for the
[coupled coefficient](fdip-00TU),
##{\Phi(P_{\gamma,M})=
 -\sum_Uw(U)\trace_{F_x}\bigl(
 M_Uh_\gamma(U)\pr_y(\adjugate K_U)\iota_x\bigr).}
This finite polynomial sum includes every link assignment.
If #{Z} is a unit, its quotient by #{Z} is the global
normalized coefficient even when an individual
#{\det K_U} is not a unit. The two-level formula of
\ref{fdip-00TX} has the stronger hypothesis that every
background kernel can also be normalized.}

\p{A Wilson line with matter ends uses an open path and
the two odd endpoint fields. A
[Wilson loop](fdip-006N) contracts a closed transport by
a fiber trace without those fields. Their gauge cancellations
use the same endpoint identity, but their coefficients
are different observables.}
